{"name":"Majolica Earthenware","hex":"#976352","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"majolica","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Earthenware decorated with coloured lead silicate glazes applied directly to an unglazed body, a process invented in Victorian England.","Earthenware coated with opaque white tin-glaze, decorated with coloured metal oxide enamel, first created in Renaissance Europe."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/majolica"},{"term":"earthenware","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An opaque, semi-porous ceramic made from clay and other compounds."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/earthenware"}],"entries":[{"title":"Majolica","blocks":[{"text":"In different periods of time and in different countries, the term majolica has been used for two distinct types of pottery."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Minton%27s_Majolica_peacock%2C_Ashmolean.jpg/330px-Minton%27s_Majolica_peacock%2C_Ashmolean.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Majolica","wikidata":"Q16867475"},{"title":"Earthenware","blocks":[{"text":"Earthenware is glazed or unglazed nonvitreous pottery that has normally been fired below 1,200 °C (2,190 °F). Basic earthenware, often called terracotta, absorbs liquids such as water. However, earthenware can be made impervious to liquids by coating it with a ceramic glaze, and such a process is used for the great majority of modern domestic earthenware. The main other important types of pottery are porcelain, bone china, and stoneware, all fired at high enough temperatures to vitrify. End applications include tableware and decorative ware such as figurines."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Samanid_plate._Attributed_to_Nishapur%2C_Iran%2C_10th_century_%28Metropolitan_Museum_of_Art%2C_67.178.2%29.jpg/330px-Samanid_plate._Attributed_to_Nishapur%2C_Iran%2C_10th_century_%28Metropolitan_Museum_of_Art%2C_67.178.2%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earthenware","wikidata":"Q1377111"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/MintonStandAndFlowerVase.jpg/500px-MintonStandAndFlowerVase.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/MintonStandAndFlowerVase.jpg/500px-MintonStandAndFlowerVase.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"English tin-glazed majolica. First shown at the 1851 Exhibition by Minton & Co., Exhibit Number 74. Potteries Museum, Stoke-on-Trent, UK","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMintonStandAndFlowerVase.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/P._396%2C_Ceramic_Manufacturers%2C_Lecture_23%2C_1852%2C_Leon_Arnoux%2C_Mintons.jpg/500px-P._396%2C_Ceramic_Manufacturers%2C_Lecture_23%2C_1852%2C_Leon_Arnoux%2C_Mintons.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/P._396%2C_Ceramic_Manufacturers%2C_Lecture_23%2C_1852%2C_Leon_Arnoux%2C_Mintons.jpg/1280px-P._396%2C_Ceramic_Manufacturers%2C_Lecture_23%2C_1852%2C_Leon_Arnoux%2C_Mintons.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/P._396%2C_Ceramic_Manufacturers%2C_Lecture_23%2C_1852%2C_Leon_Arnoux%2C_Mintons.jpg/500px-P._396%2C_Ceramic_Manufacturers%2C_Lecture_23%2C_1852%2C_Leon_Arnoux%2C_Mintons.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Leon Arnoux, 1853, describing Minton's tin-glazed product.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AP._396%2C_Ceramic_Manufacturers%2C_Lecture_23%2C_1852%2C_Leon_Arnoux%2C_Mintons.jpg"},{"srcset":"https:/